Name: toolbarHeight

Type: property

Syntax: get the toolbarHeight of <toolbar>

Summary:
The height of a toolbar in pixels, as reported by the platform.

Associations: toolbar

Introduced: 1.0.0

OS: mac, windows, linux

Platforms: desktop

Example:
-- Position card content below the toolbar
set the top of group "content" to the toolbarHeight of toolbar 1

Example:
-- Store the height for later use
put the toolbarHeight of toolbar "mainBar" into tBarHeight

Example:
-- Guard against the toolbar not yet being open
if the toolbarHeight of toolbar 1 > 0 then
   set the top of field "body" to the toolbarHeight of toolbar 1
end if

Value:
An integer giving the toolbar's current height in pixels. Returns 0 if
the toolbar has not yet been opened (i.e. its stack is not open).

Description:
Use <toolbarHeight> to determine how many pixels the toolbar occupies at
the top of the stack window so that card content can be positioned below
it without overlap.

The value is read-only and is derived directly from the platform layer:

**macOS** — the gap between the window frame rect and the content rect,
which AppKit computes automatically based on the unified toolbar height.

**Windows** — the height of the toolbar HWND as calculated by
`TB_AUTOSIZE`, which accounts for the current button size, icon size,
and system DPI.

**Linux** — once the toolbar is open, the height of the underlying
GdkWindow child. Before the first open, the value is obtained by querying
a temporary GtkToolbar widget for its natural height in the active GTK
theme. This means the result reflects per-desktop-environment
customisation (KDE, XFCE, GNOME) and HiDPI scale factors automatically,
rather than returning a fixed default.

Because the height is platform-determined it may change if the user
switches desktop themes or display scale while the application is running.
Re-read the property after a `resizeStack` or theme-change notification
rather than caching it indefinitely.

References: toolbar (object), toolbarVisible (property),
toolbarDisplayMode (property), getToolbarTopY (glossary)

Tags: ui
